mode: "100644"
text: "# Carousel Architecture\n\nThe shape of a carousel and the structures that fit different ideas. A carousel is a sequence —\nlike a thread, but visual — so the same retention logic applies: every slide must earn the next\nswipe.\n\n## The shape\n\n**Cover (hook + promise) → Body slides (the build) → Payoff slide → CTA slide.**\n\n- **Cover** — hooks and promises the swipe; the most important slide; what stops the scroll.\n- **Body** — the substance, one idea per slide, maintaining momentum.\n- **Payoff** — the summary, the result, the \"so what.\"\n- **CTA** — one ask, usually save (carousels win on saves).\n\n## The spine — outline before designing\n\nOutline the **spine** first: the cover's promise, then the ordered slides that deliver it. If you\ncan't state it in a sentence (\"cover promises 5 hooks → one hook per slide → summary → save\"), the\ncarousel isn't ready. The spine is what makes it a cohesive piece instead of unrelated slides.\n\n## One idea per carousel, one idea per slide\n\n- **Per carousel:** one teaching point or one story. Two ideas = two carousels.\n- **Per slide:** one headline-sized idea. If a slide needs a paragraph to explain, split it into\n  two slides. The constraint *is* the craft — it forces clarity.\n\n## Carousel structures — pick what fits\n\n- **How-to / step-by-step** — \"How to X.\" Cover promises the outcome; each slide = one step; payoff\n  shows the result. High save value.\n- **Listicle** — \"N ways / tips / mistakes.\" Cover promises the list; one item per slide. The\n  easiest to follow and the most save-able.\n- **Story / narrative** — a journey with tension. Cover drops in; each slide is a beat; payoff is\n  the resolution/lesson.\n- **Before → after** — the gap and how to cross it. Cover shows the contrast; slides walk the\n  transformation.\n- **Myth-bust** — \"X things you believe about Y that are wrong.\" Cover states the provocation; each\n  slide flips one myth.\n- **Framework / breakdown** — teach a model or deconstruct something. Cover names it; slides are the\n  components.\n\nMatch structure to idea: a process is a how-to; a journey is a story; a set of tips is a listicle.\n\n## Swipe momentum\n\nAttention leaks at every swipe. Counter it:\n\n- **Progress cues** — \"1/7\", or visual progress — orient and signal there's more.\n- **Forward pulls** — a slide can tee up the next (\"but the 3rd one changes everything →\").\n- **The arrow / swipe cue** on the cover and early slides (\"→\").\n\nDon't withhold value cheaply; each slide should be useful *and* pull forward.\n\n## Slide count — tight beats padded\n\nInstagram allows up to 20; LinkedIn PDFs more. But the rule is **every slide earns\nits place**. A 6-slide carousel that's all signal beats a 10-slide one padded to hit a number. Let\nthe idea set the count. If you're adding slides to \"make it longer,\" stop.\n\n## Save-optimization\n\nCarousels are saved more than any other format — design for it. Reference-value content (checklists,\nframeworks, step-by-steps, swipe-able resources) gets saved and re-opened, which signals quality.\nMake the carousel something worth keeping, and the CTA \"save this for later\" lands honestly.\n"
